572b72" :answer-field/key "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "112c3205-5323-5719-a0c7-431fff65b0ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127920" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=-1 \\\\ 3y-x &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The coefficient matrix is not  invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero” is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69107280-70c4-5ca4-8b64-1e64450165b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448278} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f6955-4016-59d3-9db9-3955354a9810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a3f6955-4016-59d3-9db9-3955354a9810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448279} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee137d6-1832-58d0-85d5-575e29a0fa26" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bde20c-189c-54ef-b19c-9e8950c65826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448280} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9158da-7221-5175-b881-fdd36f8f42ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bde20c-189c-54ef-b19c-9e8950c65826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448280} :answer-field/id #uuid "de466e8f-4f63-50c6-b72f-38c520f95211" :answer-field/key "$Ax=0$ has no solutions" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c45389-9701-5934-b1ac-2f11edac2800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448282} {:answer/id #uuid "f20f2e0e-7487-58ee-9feb-810b44ca227a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20f2e0e-7487-58ee-9feb-810b44ca227a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448283} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9964be-6910-5d74-bd0f-7b3cfcffc16d" :answer-field/key "The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "364a18ef-2de8-57d7-9e2f-631eb4651446" :question/math-academy-id "q-127931" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has no solutions” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point” is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point” are true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/title "Quiz 2" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494300} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495207}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1c76d6-26c8-504d-9404-70d3044b6d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448284} {:answer/id #uuid "52a42a90-a2f2-53a1-a38b-f3fbd471febf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a42a90-a2f2-53a1-a38b-f3fbd471febf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f5a7d6-794e-5b2e-8232-2ef8047378b7" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a25b72a-8f61-5c4f-851c-aafa09b763a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448286} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf17fa0-ce5e-57ed-be01-382386d4d6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf17fa0-ce5e-57ed-be01-382386d4d6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448287} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fe4db0-076c-5d37-abdd-95681679518e" :answer-field/key "$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f82e7b-d0c9-554d-b199-c9cd2d9d3e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448288} {:answer/id #uuid "d21d1cbd-a4f9-59d3-90e9-eebe3afbe948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f82e7b-d0c9-554d-b199-c9cd2d9d3e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4af399e-f753-5679-8b6d-af7bd231a29f" :answer-field/key "${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98a38053-dfdc-5995-9d99-1db68e6c6765" :question/math-academy-id "q-127943" :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$” is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$” are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-acade