r-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "604a5500-7093-5d2f-8ca4-b58165195b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463728} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86cafd3-543c-5143-b0b5-69b93db87424" :answer-field/key "$(0,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787a1177-1769-5956-b6cd-3399afd3dc9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129221" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)?$   \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1,2]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id 